* A nested accumulator: `Rev` carries a second argument that it *rebuilds* at
* every step, so the recursion is not a descent on one argument.
*
*   <Rev (a b c) ()>  =  (c b a)
*
* Driving it with the compiler's sequence partition (`[]` / `s.H e.T` /
* `(e.B) e.T`) leaves `Rev` entirely residual: the partition peels the *tail*
* of the argument list and never enters the bracket `(e.B)`, so nothing folds —
* measured at residual-work 37, worse than the source's own structure. The
* constructor-entering partition (`--split pattern`) partitions the bracket's
* *contents* by the callee's own patterns, so `Rev` folds into a `Split1` that
* carries its recursion — measured at residual-work 9 with `Rev` eliminated.
*
* This is E-11's accumulator case: the behaviour SCP4 1999 attributes to the
* two-level stack configuration, reached here by entering the constructor
* rather than by adding an explicit control stack.

$ENTRY Go {
  e.X = <Rev e.X ()>;
}

Rev {
  () (e.A) = (e.A);
  (t.H e.T) (e.A) = <Rev (e.T) (t.H e.A)>;
}
